'''Siddig el-Fadil''' (born 21 November 1965), known professionally as '''Alexander Siddig''', is a Sudanese-born <!--DO NOT CHANGE NATIONALTIY.-->British<!--DISCUSS ON TALK PAGE BEFORE CHANGING.--> actor and director. Siddig is best known for his roles as [[Julian Bashir|Dr. Julian Bashir]] in the television series ''[[Star Trek: Deep Space Nine]]'', former terrorist [[Hamri Al-Assad]] in the [[24 (season 6)|sixth season]] of the series ''[[24 (TV series)|24]]'', [[Doran Martell]] in ''[[Game of Thrones]]'',  [[Ra's al Ghul]] in ''[[Gotham (TV series)|Gotham]]'', and Philip Burton in ''[[Primeval (TV series)|Primeval]]''.  He also starred in the films ''[[Syriana]]'' (2005), ''[[Hannibal (2006 TV film)|Hannibal]]'' (2006), ''[[A Lost Man]]'' (2007), ''[[Cairo Time]]'' (2009), and ''[[Inescapable (film)|Inescapable]]'' (2012).

==Early life ==
Siddig el-Tahir el-Fadil el-Siddig Abdurrahman Mohammed Ahmed Abdel Karim el-Mahdi ({{langx|ar|صدّيق الطاهر الفاضل الصدّيق عبدالرحمن محمد أحمد عبدالكريم المهدي|Ṣiddīq aṭ-Ṭāhir al-Fāḍil aṣ-Ṣiddīq ʿAbd ar-Raḥman Muḥammad ʾAḥmad ʿAbd al-Karīm al-Mahdī}}) was born on 21 November 1965 in [[Omdurman]], [[Republic of Sudan (1956–1969)|Republic of Sudan]] (present-day, [[Sudan]]) to a Sudanese father, Tahir El Mahdi, and Gloria Birkett (née Taylor; d. 2001), an English model and theatrical press agent.<ref name=BIDOUN>{{cite magazine |last=Mahjoub |first=Jamal |date=Summer 2009 |title=Alexander Siddig: The Accidental Arab |url=https://www.bidoun.org/articles/alexander-siddig |magazine=[[Bidoun]] |issue=18 |location=Brooklyn, New York, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref name=HUFFPO>{{cite web |url=https://www.huffpost.com/entry/alexander-siddig-discover_b_817826 |title=Alexander Siddig — Discovering the Uncommon Hero |last=Rothe |first=E. Nina | website=[[HuffPost]] |date=3 February 2011|access-date=2 May 2020}}</ref><ref name=CHICAGOTRIBUNE>{{cite news |last=Spelling |first=Ian |date=31 December 1993 |title=El Fadil having time of his life as a jerk |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/news/ct-xpm-1993-12-31-9312310158-story.html |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |location=Chicago, Illinois, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref name=OKLAHOMAN>{{cite news |last=Cole |first=Maxine |date=2 August 1998 |title=Your Page |url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/454667430/ |work=[[The Daily Oklahoman]] TV This Week |location=Oklahoma City, Oklahoma, United States |page=17 |access-date=20 April 2020 |via=Newspapers.com |url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref name=PLACEOFBIRTH>{{cite news |last=Harris |first=Will |date=19 July 2015 |title=Alexander Siddig on being Bashir, quitting 24, and getting saddle-sore for Ridley Scott |url=https://www.avclub.com/alexander-siddig-on-being-bashir-quitting-24-and-gett-1798282506 |work=[[The A.V. Club]] |location=Chicago, Illinois, United States |access-date=20 April 2020 |quote=So I did that, and then I was also born in Omdurman, which is in Sudan, which is where the confluence of the Nile, where this great river passes through, so I’m intimately acquainted with the most important thing about ancient Egypt, which is the river.}}</ref><ref name=GUARDIAN>{{cite news |last=Burton |first=Humphrey |date=18 May 2015 |title=Lord Birkett obituary |url=https://www.theguardian.com/film/2015/may/18/lord-birkett-obituary |work=[[The Guardian]] |location=London, United Kingdom |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref name=GLORIA>{{cite news |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=21 July 2015 |title=Lord Birkett: Film producer who trained at Ealing Studios and championed the arts |url=https://www.thetimes.com/travel/destinations/uk-travel/england/london-travel/lord-birkett-x9ws8pqzp3p |work=[[The Times]] |location=London, United Kingdom |access-date=20 April 2020|url-access=subscription}}</ref> 

Through his father, Siddig is the nephew to [[Sadiq al-Mahdi]], Prime Minister of Sudan during [[List_of_heads_of_government_of_Sudan#Republic_of_Sudan_(1956–1969)|1966{{en dash}}1967]] and [[List_of_heads_of_government_of_Sudan#Republic_of_Sudan_(1985–2019)|1986{{en dash}}1989]], and the filmmaker, poet and painter [[Hussein Shariffe]].<ref name=BIDOUN/><ref name=MIRROR>{{cite news |last=Keegan |first=Simon |date=15 May 2015 |title=Game of Thrones star Alexander Siddig is descendant of real dynasty of despots |url=https://www.mirror.co.uk/tv/tv-news/game-thrones-star-alexander-siddig-5701748 |work=[[Daily Mirror]] |location=London, United Kingdom |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> Siddig is the great-great-grandson of [[Muhammad Ahmad]], a [[Nubians|Nubian]] religious leader and founder of the [[Mahdist State]] who was proclaimed the [[Mahdi]] by his disciples.<ref name=BIDOUN/><ref name=MIRROR/> Through his mother, Siddig is the nephew of actor [[Malcolm McDowell]] and cousin of film director and screenwriter [[Charlie McDowell]].<ref name=BIDOUN/><ref name=PEOPLE>{{cite magazine |last=Lipton |first=Michael A. |date=15 July 1996 |title=A Family Enterprise |url=https://people.com/archive/a-family-enterprise-vol-46-no-3/ |magazine=[[People (magazine)|People]] |volume=43 |issue=3 |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref>

Siddig's parents met in the 1960s when his mother travelled to Sudan with a friend, who introduced her to Siddig's father.<ref name=BIDOUN/> Initially living in Sudan, in 1967 the family relocated to London.<ref name=BIDOUN/><ref name=CHICAGOTRIBUNE/> Siddig initially spoke [[Arabic]] when he was a child, but by his second year of living in Britain, he had forgotten much of it.<ref name=BIDOUN/> On the subject of his childhood, he stated in 2024, "I had a tough time at school and in my teens because being not-white in the UK during the 70s was prickly and you had to sort of negotiate that. I tried to push it aside, my race and my heritage … I would have had to go out of my way to be ethnic."<ref name=comicsbeat/> In 1978, Siddig's mother married film director and producer [[Michael Birkett]], who adopted Siddig. [[Thomas Birkett, 3rd Baron Birkett|Thomas Birkett]] (b. 1982) is Siddig's half-brother.<ref name=GUARDIAN/>

Siddig attended [[St Lawrence College, Ramsgate]].<ref name=SCHOOL>{{cite magazine |last=Mack |first=Peter |date=1981 |title=A Midsummer Night's Dream: A Review of the Production |magazine=The Lawrentian, Vol.LXXXI, No.1 |location=Ramsgate, Kent, UK |quote=Siddig El Fadil as Puck was full of energy and mischief.}}</ref> He also studied geography and anthropology for a year at [[University College London]] before enrolling at the [[London Academy of Music and Dramatic Art]] (LAMDA).<ref name=CHICAGOTRIBUNE/><ref name=COLLIDER>{{cite web |url=https://collider.com/alexander-siddig-interview-cairo-time-deep-space-nine-miral/ |title=Alexander Siddig Exclusive Interview |last=Messer |first=Ron |date=9 August 2010 |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]] |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref>

==Career==
Siddig's first acting role was at the age of 14 in a non-speaking role, playing [[King Tut]] in a [[BBC]] children's production.<ref name=PLACEOFBIRTH /> After leaving LAMDA, Siddig worked in theatre as both an actor and director.<ref name=CHICAGOTRIBUNE/><ref name=COLLIDER/><ref name=AVCLUB>{{cite news |last=Harris |first=Will |date=19 July 2015 |title=Alexander Siddig on being Bashir, quitting 24, and getting saddle-sore for Ridley Scott |url=https://www.avclub.com/alexander-siddig-on-being-bashir-quitting-24-and-gett-1798282506 |work=[[The A.V. Club]] |location=Chicago, Illinois, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> Siddig's first television role was a [[Palestinians|Palestinian]] man in a British six-part miniseries called ''The Big Battalions'' (filmed in 1989 but released in 1992), and shortly afterward, he won the role of Prince Feisal in ''[[A Dangerous Man: Lawrence After Arabia]]'', a 1990 telefilm sequel to ''[[Lawrence of Arabia (film)|Lawrence of Arabia]]'' starring [[Ralph Fiennes]].<ref name=CHICAGOTRIBUNE/><ref name=AVCLUB/>

Siddig's role in ''A Dangerous Man: Lawrence After Arabia'' brought him to the attention of Rick Berman, executive producer of ''[[Star Trek: Deep Space Nine]]'' (1993).<ref name=CHICAGOTRIBUNE/> Berman originally considered Siddig for the role of Commander [[Benjamin Sisko]], but decided in the end that Siddig was too young for the role and cast him as Dr. [[Julian Bashir]].<ref name=CHICAGOTRIBUNE/>  Siddig remained with ''Deep Space Nine'' for all seven seasons of the series.<ref name=BIDOUN/> He also directed the episodes "[[Business as Usual (Star Trek: Deep Space Nine)|Business as Usual]]" (1997) and "[[Profit and Lace]]" (1998).<ref name=DIRECTINGDS9>{{cite news |last=Spelling |first=Ian |date=3 April 1997 |title=Directing 'DS9' Takes Siddig To A Higher Plane |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/news/ct-xpm-1997-04-03-9704030277-story.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |via=[[Chicago Tribune]] |location=New York City, New York, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Handlen |first=Zack |date=16 January 2014 |title=Star Trek: Deep Space Nine: "Valiant"/"Profit And Lace" |url=https://www.avclub.com/star-trek-deep-space-nine-valiant-profit-and-lace-1798179205 |work=[[The A.V. Club]] |location=Chicago, Illinois, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> In 1995, Siddig also changed his stage name from Siddig El Fadil to Alexander Siddig.<ref name=OKLAHOMAN/>

After the completion of ''Deep Space Nine'' in 1999, Siddig began directing a film about the [[Soviet]] [[Night Witches]].<ref name="sidcity1999">{{cite web |last1=Siddig |first1=Alexander |title=Letters from Siddig: July 1999 |website=SidCity.net |date=27 October 2008 |publisher=Sid City |url=https://sidcity.net/?page_id=112}}</ref> The film was never completed.<ref name="assignmentx">{{cite web |last1=Bernstein |first1=Abbie |title=GOTHAM: Alexander Siddig talks the return of Ra al Ghul – exclusive interview |url=https://www.assignmentx.com/2018/gotham-actor-alexander-siddig-exclusive-interview/ |website=Assignment X |date=22 March 2018 }}</ref> Siddig told ''[[Bidoun]]'' magazine that demand for Islamic and Arabic character roles was increasing in both film and television after the [[September 11 attacks]] and that people began to approach him with projects within six months of the event.<ref name=BIDOUN/> He played a mountain guide in the thriller film ''[[Vertical Limit]]'' (2000), starring [[Chris O'Donnell]], and Ajay in the postapocalyptic science-fantasy film ''[[Reign of Fire (film)|Reign of Fire]]'' (2002) starring [[Christian Bale]].<ref name=BIDOUN/><ref name=AVCLUB/> In 2003, Siddig played the role of an [[Demographics of Algeria|Algerian]] secret agent on the trail of Islamists in the controversial episode "[[Nest of Angels]]" of the British television show ''[[Spooks (TV series)|Spooks]]'' (known as ''MI-5'' in the US).<ref>{{cite news |last=Leonard |first=Tom |date=11 June 2003 |title=Bomber on ''Spooks'' upsets 1,000 viewers |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1432641/Bomber-on-Spooks-upsets-1000-viewers.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/uknews/1432641/Bomber-on-Spooks-upsets-1000-viewers.html |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |location=London, United Kingdom |access-date=20 April 2020}}{{cbignore}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=10 June 2003 |title=Viewers upset by Muslim plot |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/entertainment/2978414.stm |work=[[BBC News Online]] |location=London, United Kingdom |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> Siddig appeared in a [[Cameo appearance|cameo]] role as [[Khalid Sheikh Mohammed]] in the film ''[[The Hamburg Cell (film)|The Hamburg Cell]]'' that premiered the following year.<ref name=BIDOUN/>

In 2005, Siddig returned to the live stage, playing the role of Doctor Scott in a production of ''[[Whose Life Is It Anyway? (play)|Whose Life Is It Anyway?]]'' starring [[Kim Cattrall]] that played at the [[Comedy Theatre]] in London.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Inverne |first=James |date=7 January 2005 |title=Kim Cattrall Starts London Previews Jan. 7 in Whose Life Is It Anyway? |url=https://www.playbill.com/article/kim-cattrall-starts-london-previews-jan-7-in-whose-life-is-it-anyway-com-123454 |magazine=[[Playbill]] |location=New York City, New York, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.londontheatre.co.uk/theatre-news/news/whose-life-is-it-anywayfull-cast-confirmed |title="Whose Life is it Anyway?"Full cast confirmed! |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=25 October 2004 |website=londontheatre.co.uk |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> That same year, Siddig appeared as Saladin's aide [[Imad ad-Din al-Isfahani|Imad]] in [[Ridley Scott]]'s 2005 film ''[[Kingdom of Heaven (film)|Kingdom of Heaven]]'',<ref name=AVCLUB/><ref>{{cite news |last=Moore |first=Roger |date=6 May 2005 |title=Thrills but not enough soul |url=https://www.orlandosentinel.com/news/os-xpm-2005-05-06-0505050423-story.html |work=[[Orlando Sentinel]] |location=Orlando, Florida, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> and starred alongside [[George Clooney]] and [[Matt Damon]] as Prince Nasir in the film ''[[Syriana]]''.<ref name=BIDOUN/><ref>{{cite magazine |last=McCarthy |first=Todd |date=19 November 2005 |title=Syriana |url=https://variety.com/2005/scene/markets-festivals/syriana-2-1200520184/ |magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |location=Los Angeles, California, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref>

Siddig played the title role in the BBC's 2006 telefilm ''[[Hannibal (2006 TV film)|Hannibal]]''.<ref>{{cite news <!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |date=9 May 2006 |title=Hannibal |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/programmes/breakfast/4753017.stm |work=[[BBC News]] |location=London, United Kingdom |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> In 2007, he starred in ''[[A Lost Man]]'' (French title ''Un Homme Perdu'') a French-language film that screened at the [[Cannes Film Festival]].<ref name=AVCLUB/><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Jaafar |first=Ali |date=1 May 2007 |title='Lost Man' finds Cannes slot |url=https://variety.com/2007/scene/markets-festivals/lost-man-finds-cannes-slot-1117964057/ |magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |location=Los Angeles, California, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> That same year, he played the role of former terrorist Hamri Al-Assad in the [[24 (season 6)|sixth season]] of ''[[24 (TV series)|24]]''.<ref name=STAR/><ref name=AVCLUB/>

In 2009, he co-starred with [[Patricia Clarkson]] in the film ''[[Cairo Time]]'' as Tareq Khalifa, an Egyptian who battles his attraction to his friend's wife.<ref name=STAR>{{cite news |last=Ouzounian |first=Richard |date=10 October 2009 |title=Stars sparkle on Cairo Time |url=https://www.thestar.com/news/2009/10/10/stars_sparkle_on_cairo_time.html |work=[[Toronto Star]] |location=Toronto, Ontario, Canada |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Taylor |first=Ella |date=5 August 2010 |title=Looking For Love On 'Cairo Time'? Prepare To Wait |url=https://www.npr.org/2010/08/05/128976065/looking-for-love-on-cairo-time-prepare-to-wait |work=[[NPR]] |location=Washington, District of Columbia, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> Directed by Canadian director [[Ruba Nadda]], the film won the prize for "Best Canadian Feature Film" at the [[2009 Toronto International Film Festival]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://tiff.net/thefestival/about/awards/awards2009 |title=2009 Toronto International Film Festival Winners |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131112210849/http://tiff.net/thefestival/about/awards/awards2009 |website=tiff.net |archive-date=12 November 2013}}</ref> He also played Jamal in [[Julian Schnabel]]’s 2010 film ''[[Miral]]''.<ref name=HUFFPO/>

Siddig played Philip Burton in series four and five of the ITV science-fiction drama programme ''[[Primeval (TV series)|Primeval]]''; both series aired in 2011.<ref name=HUFFPO/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.digitalspy.com/tv/cult/a291926/primeval-week-qa-with-alexander-siddig/ |title='Primeval' Week: Q&A with Alexander Siddig |last1=Wightman |first1=Catriona |last2=Mansell |first2=Tom |date=26 December 2010 |website=[[Digital Spy]] |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> In 2012, Siddig again worked with director Nadda and played the starring role as a Syrian-Canadian businessman in the film ''[[Inescapable (film)|Inescapable]]'', which also starred [[Marisa Tomei]] and [[Joshua Jackson]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Arrazola |first=Luis-Enrique |date=16 March 2012 |title=All surprisingly calm on the set of Inescapable |url=https://nationalpost.com/entertainment/all-surprisingly-calm-on-the-set-of-inescapable |work=[[National Post]] |location=Toronto, Ontario, Canada |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Schneller |first=Johanna |date=14 September 2012 |title=Inescapable: Anatomy of a DIY blockbuster |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/arts/awards-and-festivals/tiff/inescapable-anatomy-of-a-diy-blockbuster/article4545263/ |work=[[The Globe and Mail]] |location=Toronto, Ontario, Canada |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref>

In 2013, Siddig narrated the [[BBC Two]] nature documentary series ''[[Wild Arabia]]'' and played the role of [[Minos]] in the first series of the BBC [[fantasy]]-[[Adventure film|adventure]] programme ''[[Atlantis (TV series)|Atlantis]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last=Wollaston |first=Sam |date=22 February 2013 |title=Last night's TV: Wild Arabia – review |url=https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2013/feb/22/last-nights-tv-wild-arabia-review |work=[[The Guardian]] |location=London, United Kingdom |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Gelt |first=Jessica |date=27 November 2013 |title=BBC America picks up second season of fantasy show 'Atlantis' |url=https://www.latimes.com/entertainment/tv/showtracker/la-et-st-bbc-america-second-season-atlantis-20131127-story.html |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |location=Los Angeles, California, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref>

From 2013 to 2015, Siddig played Aslan Al-Rahim ("the Turk") in the BBC [[historical fantasy]] drama series ''[[Da Vinci's Demons]]''.<ref name=AVCLUB/><ref>{{cite news |last=Truitt |first=Brian |date=7 October 2015 |title=Sneak peek: Da Vinci, mom in 'Demons' |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/tv/2015/10/07/sneak-peek-da-vincis-demons/73471476/ |work=[[USA Today]] |location=McLean, Virginia, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> In 2015 and 2016, he appeared in the [[HBO]] series ''[[Game of Thrones]]''; Siddig played the role of [[Doran Martell]], the ruling Prince of Dorne, in the series' fifth and sixth seasons.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Robinson |first=Joanna |date=23 September 2016 |title=Game of Thrones Actor Gives Scorching Exit Interview |url=https://www.vanityfair.com/hollywood/2016/09/game-of-thrones-alexander-siddig-interview-leaked-game-of-thrones-screeners |magazine=[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]] |location=New York City, New York, United States |publisher=[[Condé Nast]] |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> He also played Reuben Oliver, an artist and suitor of Polly Shelby, in the third season of the British series ''[[Peaky Blinders (TV series)|Peaky Blinders]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last=Hughes |first=Sarah |date=5 May 2016 |title=Peaky Blinders recap: series three, episode one – welcome back boys! |url=https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2016/may/05/peaky-blinders-recap-series-three-episode-one-welcome-back-boys |work=[[The Guardian]] |location=London, United Kingdom |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> Siddig also provided the voice of Wolf, a character in the historical fantasy drama ''[[Tumanbay (radio drama)|Tumanbay]]'' that aired on [[BBC Radio 4]] between December 2015 and February 2016.

In 2017, Siddig joined the cast of the televisions series ''[[Gotham (TV series)|Gotham]]'' as [[League of Shadows]] leader [[Ra’s al Ghul]]<ref name=CHICAGOTRIBUNE2>{{cite news |last=Phillips |first=Jevon |date=2 March 2017 |title='Gotham' finds its Ra's Al Ghul in 'Game of Thrones' actor Alexander Siddig |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/la-et-entertainment-news-updates-march-gotham-finds-its-ra-s-al-ghul-in-1488477923-htmlstory.html |work=[[Chicago Tribune]] |location=Chicago, Illinois, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Bryant |first=Jacob |date=2 March 2017 |title='Gotham' Casts 'Star Trek' Alum Alexander Siddig as Ra's al Ghul |url=https://variety.com/2017/tv/news/alexander-siddig-gotham-ras-al-ghul-1202000348/ |magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |location=Los Angeles, California, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> and also played the part of [[Aristotle Onassis]] in the drama miniseries ''[[The Kennedys: After Camelot]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last=Shanahan |first=Mark |date=13 June 2016 |title=Katie Holmes on the set of 'The Kennedys: After Camelot' |url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/lifestyle/names/2016/06/13/katie-holmes-jackie-onassis-set-the-kennedys-after-camelot/aTHxVEWglEV8lbPFd1xy7L/story.html |work=[[The Boston Globe]] |location=Boston, Massachusetts, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref>

In 2019, he appeared in the [[Netflix]] series ''[[The Spy (TV series)|The Spy]]'' as Ahmed Suidani, a Syrian security officer.<ref>{{cite news |last=Rabinowitz |first=Dorothy |date=5 September 2019 |title='The Spy' Review: A Life Undercover |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-spy-review-a-life-undercover-11567715482 |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |location=New York City, New York, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Sepinwall |first=Alan |date=3 September 2019 |title='The Spy' Review: Sacha Baron Cohen Goes Undercover — This Time Not for Laughs |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/tv/tv-reviews/spy-netflix-sacha-baron-cohen-review-875869/ |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]] |location=New York City, New York, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref> That same year, he appeared as Issouf Al Moctar in the second season of the [[Fox (British and Irish TV channel)|Fox (UK and Ireland)]] thriller series ''[[Deep State (TV series)|Deep State]]'', and voiced Avi Singh in ''[[Star Wars: The Bad Batch]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.behindthevoiceactors.com/tv-shows/Star-Wars-The-Bad-Batch/Senator-Avi-Singh/|title=Senator Avi Singh Voice|website=Behind the Voice Actors|access-date=March 15, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220610205958/https://www.behindthevoiceactors.com/tv-shows/Star-Wars-The-Bad-Batch/Senator-Avi-Singh/|archive-date=June 10, 2022|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Siddig began a relationship with [[Nana Visitor]] whilst working on the set of ''Star Trek: Deep Space Nine''.<ref name=LOVE>{{cite news |last=O'Hare |first=Kate |date=15 June 1997 |title=Love, marriage and baby carriage on 'Star Trek' |url=https://www.newspapers.com/image/304310479/ |work=[[The Times]], TV Times (insert) |location=Munster, Indiana, United States |agency=Tribune Media Services |page=19 |access-date=20 April 2020 |via=Newspapers.com |url-access=subscription}}</ref> Their son, Django El Tahir El Siddig, was born on 16 September 1996 and Visitor's pregnancy was written into the storyline of ''Deep Space Nine''.<ref name=PEOPLE/><ref name=LOVE/> Django is Siddig's first child and Visitor's second son.<ref name=LOVE/> The couple married in 1997 and divorced in 2001.<ref name=LOVE/><ref>{{cite magazine |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=10 July 2016 |title=Original to Enterprise: 50 Years of 'Star Trek' Crews |url=https://www.newsweek.com/star-trek-crew-came-beyond-478205 |magazine=[[Newsweek]] |location=New York City, New York, United States |access-date=20 April 2020}}</ref>

Siddig met his second wife, Shana Collier, while filming ''Cairo Time'', for which she was an assistant producer; the couple married in 2015.<ref name="sidcity2020">{{cite web |last1=Lowery |first1=Melissa |title=Alexander Siddig Met His Wife on the Set of Cairo Time |url=https://www.sidcity.net/2020/02/14/alexander-siddig-met-his-wife-on-the-set-of-cairo-time/ |website=SidCity.net |date=14 February 2020 |publisher=Sid City}}</ref>

Siddig stated he was neither [[Christian]] nor [[Muslim]] in a 2001 letter to his fanclub,<ref name="sidcity2001">{{cite web |last1=Siddig |first1=Alexander |title=Letters from Siddig: September 2001 |url=https://sidcity.net/?page_id=110 |website=SidCity.net |date=27 October 2008 |publisher=Sid City}}</ref> but in a 2004 letter described himself as having "quite recently (after nearly 40 years) discovered that he was Muslim".<ref name="sidcity2004">{{cite web |last1=Siddig |first1=Alexander |title=Letters from Siddig: December 2004 |url=https://sidcity.net/?page_id=106 |website=SidCity.net |date=27 October 2008 |publisher=Sid City}}</ref>

In an April 2024 interview, Siddig described his sexuality as "not quite straight".<ref name=comicsbeat>{{Cite web |last=Lloyd |first=Kelas |date=2024-04-09 |title=Interview: STAR TREK's Alexander Siddig celebrates Arab-American Heritage Month |url=https://www.comicsbeat.com/interview-star-treks-alexander-siddig-celebrates-arab-american-heritage-month/ |access-date=2024-06-25 |website=The Beat |language=en-US}}</ref> He has described his hobbies as cooking, woodworking, gardening and computer games.<ref name="sidcity2018">{{cite web |last1=Lowery |first1=Melissa |title=Alexander Siddig Answers Fan Questions: 2018 Edition |url=https://www.sidcity.net/2018/12/31/alexander-siddig-answers-fan-questions-2018-edition/ |website=SidCity.net |date=31 December 2018 |publisher=Sid City}}</ref>

While filming ''Deep Space Nine'' he resided in [[Los Angeles]]; afterwards he relocated to [[New York City]] with his family.<ref name=BIDOUN /> As of 2018, he resides in [[Massachusetts]] with his wife.<ref name="sidcity2018" />

